go on forever. So, it has to come to a halt. So, it did in 2000, but the development

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:35.130
of networks and its utility and important remains. So, now let us go to a brief history

00:13:35.130 --> 00:13:40.250
more focused on the network itself. So, that part so we were in general talking about the

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:46.790
computing scene in general. So, in mid 1960s USA department of defense

00:13:46.790 --> 00:13:52.650
wanted a command and control network that could survive a nuclear attack. Now, if

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:57.370
something had to survive a nuclear attack then you have to distribute it. So, that

00:13:57.370 --> 00:14:02.710
was one strong reason that we wanted distributed system and if you have a distributed

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:10.590
system this system must be able to communicate. So, that was the seed of this project. The

00:14:10.590 --> 00:14:16.610
subnet should consist of some Honeywell 12kw intermediate message processors. So, there

00:14:16.610 --> 00:14:23.350
are some kind of early network devices you might say which are connected by 64 kbps

00:14:23.350 --> 00:14:30.250
line. These IMPs or intermediate message processors were connected to the various computers and

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:38.910
IMPs themselves were connected by telephone lines from telephone companies. Later the

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:44.490
IMP software was changed to terminal interface processors which allowed the connection

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:50.570
of terminals. In 1970s NSF set up a machine connected to the ARPANET to which other

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:57.550
universities could develop and connect. Now, this became very important in the sense that

00:14:57.550 --> 00:15:03.810
many people were interested in this. So, NSF upgraded their system in various ways and

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:09.850
by mid 1980s NSF built a new backbone to connect with supercomputing centers to some

00:15:09.850 --> 00:15:22.000
regional network. So, this backbone was upgraded to 448 kbps and then to 1.5 mbps

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:27.100
and once this fiber backbone was there communication was really fast. So, it allowed network

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:32.680
connection to hundreds of thousands of universities, research labs, libraries, museums etcetera.

00:15:33.820 --> 00:15:40.060
So, as the growth continued now commercial houses began to take notice and to join

00:15:40.060 --> 00:15:53.360
the decentralized model began to take hold. And as I mean as I just described earlier

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:59.260
that as the commercial people joined. So, there was a lot of innovation by a lot of

00:15:59.260 --> 00:16:08.360
people. One of the major driving force in the computing network in the past decades

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:14.560
or even now may be has been the role of so called startup companies. By startup

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:20.320
companies that means that some young engineers may be or some young people they had

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:27.420
some very bright idea which they developed to a certain extent. And if when that was sort

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:33.160
of proven then some big company would possibly buy up their company or give them money to

00:16:33.160 --> 00:16:38.740
get access to this kind of technology. And a lot of people and there were so many

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:43.920
success stories that a lot of people got interested in it and when a lot of people

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:49.620
think about a problem naturally there is a lot of innovation. So, that is how computer

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:56.560
networks had an explosive growth in the whole of 90s and even in the this century there

00:16:56.560 --> 00:17:05.140
is a 21st century I mean the growth is going on. Although the frenzy in the stock

00:17:05.140 --> 00:17:11.260
exchanges etcetera about this dot com company that sort of crashed in 2000, but the

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:16.920
growth in the field of networks and it used in various walks of life that is

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:25.270
continuing. With this brief historical background now let us come to the networks

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:33.430
and some kind of a structure or some kind of an abstract view of this computer network.

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:39.810
We have I have already mentioned that computer network would be a number of nodes

00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:48.070
and nodes could be computer or nodes could be network devices also which are

00:17:48.070 --> 00:17:55.030
connected by some communication lines. Now, when I say that of course sometimes things

00:17:55.030 --> 00:18:02.430
are not so simple sometimes you do have a line between two nodes let us say. So, giving us

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:07.630
the so called point to point communication that means one node A is communicating directly

00:18:07.630 --> 00:18:14.850
to one another node B where A and B are connected by a direct line let us say it

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:18.670
could be a physically some copper line or some fiber line etcetera.

00:18:20.210 --> 00:18:25.490
So, but this point to point connection could be so in the simplest case they are actually

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:32.730
dedicated, but in the dedicated case also we have some different cases for example you

00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:38.750
could have a simplex communication by simplex communication we mean that communication can

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:45.730
go in only one direction in that line. It could be half duplex by half duplex that means

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:51.410
that is the communication could go both ways either way either from A to B or from B to

00:18:51.410 --> 00:18:58.670
A, but only in one direction at a time and full duplex of course that A and B can communicate

00:18:58.670 --> 00:19:04.650
with each other simultaneously there is parallely at the same time. So, that means

00:19:04.650 --> 00:19:10.570
there is communication from A to B and from B to A at the same time. So, these are the

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:20.310
different kinds of dedicated line then if the nodes are not say computer some network

00:19:20.310 --> 00:19:27.570
devices specifically if they are sort of multiplexer then we could share a point to

00:19:27.570 --> 00:19:34.810
point medium that means there is one line from A to B, but A is connected to a lot of other

00:19:34.810 --> 00:19:40.550
nodes let us say A 1, A 2 to A n and B is connected again to a lot of other nodes say

00:19:40.550 --> 00:19:46.290
B 1, B 2 to B n and they could all these A i's and B i's they could all communicate

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:53.230
through this one single line which is between A and B. So, this we will see later how

00:19:53.230 --> 00:20:01.810
such a thing can be done. So, this business of sharing a link over time or whatever even

00:20:01.810 --> 00:20:07.370
parallely sometimes that is possible. So, sharing this link by multiple people that is called

00:20:07.370 --> 00:20:12.950
multiplexing. So, these are the different kinds of point to point links, but point

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:17.050
to point links are not the only things we have. For example, we could have a broadcast

00:20:17.050 --> 00:20:26.450
kind of link. For example, if you have a satellite communication, a satellite sort of

00:20:26.450 --> 00:20:33.410
throws its signal all over the country or may be all over the region. So, that is something

00:20:33.410 --> 00:20:37.030
which is being broadcast everything. So, you cannot really put it down as a point

00:20:37.030 --> 00:20:43.110
to point link between something, but of course you could use that shared medium

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:51.610
and a broadcast in some way to make temporary point to point links or your application may

00:20:51.610 --> 00:20:56.470
be such that you want to broadcast something. For example, we broadcast TV signal similarly

00:20:56.470 --> 00:21:02.210
there are things you might want to broadcast all over. So, there could be. So, this broadcast

00:21:02.210 --> 00:21:08.290
medium again could be dedicated to some user or it could be shared that means it is

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:14.490
shared between multiple user. So, there the term that is used is multiple access a medium

00:21:14.490 --> 00:21:21.250
to which different users are connected at different point and it is a broadcast kind of medium.

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:27.530
So, in a distributed fashion they have some protocol of deciding how to share this because

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:33.090
if it is in the same place you can the business of sharing become somewhat easier.

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:38.810
So, you can multiply it, but if it is if the nodes themselves are distributed over this broadcast

00:21:38.810 --> 00:21:43.870
medium physically distributed that is. So, you will have to have some kind of protocol

00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:49.950
so that is called multiple access. So, you can have a shared broadcast medium also that

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:57.270
is also quite common. And then finally we have this something

00:21:57.270 --> 00:22:03.010
which is in between broadcasting and point to point which is called multicasting. This

00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:09.490
means that say we have a group I have say some friends and I want to send some things

00:22:09.490 --> 00:22:14.110
which not to one particular friend, but to my group of friends, but to know others.

00:22:14.370 --> 00:22:20.130
So, multicasting is basically giving a I mean communicating to a specified group.

00:22:26.500 --> 00:22:34.880
So, using such communication link we have this computer network and the network themselves

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:42.760
are I mean quite often popularly they are sort of divided into local area network, metropolitan

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:51.560
area network and wide area network. Local area network is just that which means that

00:22:51.560 --> 00:22:59.220
it is local that means it is sort of it is sort of limited to may be one building or may

00:22:59.220 --> 00:23:04.580
be to a small group of building. So, it is its size is small that is one thing.

00:23:04.760 --> 00:23:10.700
There are some other things about local area network which makes the issues of local

00:23:10.700 --> 00:23:15.560
area network somewhat different from a larger network. So, and that is that the local

00:23:15.560 --> 00:23:21.340
area network would usually be privately owned. Now, how does ownership come into picture?

00:23:21.880 --> 00:23:28.920
Ownership comes into picture because if you have a same owner for the entire network then

00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:34.620
you can have the same policy. So, which is not the case if it is a very

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:38.220
wide area network, we will come to that wide area network that is the other case

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:43.200
where there may be I mean the different parts of I mean there are various nodes

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:48.180
which are connected to this wide area network. The nodes may belong to very different

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:52.280
people and they may have very different ideas about what should be done and about

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:57.900
all their policies. So, that makes that wide area networking somewhat different

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:04.180
from local area networking and in between we have this metropolitan area

00:24:04.180 --> 00:24:09.680
network. Metropolitan area means say a network which is spread over a community

00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:16.500
or maybe even a city. So, there are bigger sizes than local area network and there

00:24:16.500 --> 00:24:21.940
one of the issues which is very important is the access issues which means that how

00:24:21.940 --> 00:24:28.820
do you connect for example, you want to make an entire community network. So, how

00:24:28.820 --> 00:24:33.240
do you connect each one of them because they are geographically more distributed

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:37.600
than a local area network. Local area network maybe you would like to sometimes

00:24:37.600 --> 00:24:42.820
you would use welders sometimes you would just simply draw weld and the cost will not

00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:48.420
be very prohibitive, but in local area how we connect the people that becomes an

00:24:48.420 --> 00:24:53.040
issue. So, that is the access issue and we have talked about the wide area network.

00:24:53.220 --> 00:24:58.960
Wide area networks are costly that means naturally when you are talking

00:24:58.960 --> 00:25:05.780
about communicating over maybe hundreds or thousands of kilometers. The communication

00:25:05.780 --> 00:25:10.720
is costly the cost has come down and that is another thing which is historically

00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:16.780
happened as more and more people got interested in the network technology

00:25:16.780 --> 00:25:20.240
developed that is one side of the issue the other side of the issue is that the

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:24.620
volumes went up which means that the number of people who used the

00:25:24.620 --> 00:25:30.320
network. So, the as the volumes went up the cost went down. So, the technology

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:35.840
improved on in one direction. So, that was one input into bringing down the cost

00:25:35.840 --> 00:25:42.040
and the other issue was that more and more people started using it. So, the volume

00:25:42.040 --> 00:25:47.040
to a particular company let us say developing something in the network area.

00:25:47.300 --> 00:25:51.600
So, that went up. So, they could make it make things cheaper and as the

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:55.060
whole thing becomes cheaper and cheaper more and more people wanted to get

00:25:55.060 --> 00:26:01.280
to join the network. But wide area network cost is still an issue and of course,

00:26:01.340 --> 00:26:06.420
wide area network as I said that it is a it may connect various local area

00:26:06.420 --> 00:26:10.040
networks. So, it is a network of networks or internet

00:26:10.040 --> 00:26:16.720
work in or I mean as we know it very popularly that is the internet.

00:26:19.980 --> 00:26:31.820
Now, let us look at abstract idea about the abstract idea about the network in

00:26:31.820 --> 00:26:39.240
general. So, in the formal framework we have what are known as protocols. Now,

00:26:39.300 --> 00:26:43.860
why do we need protocols in network? We need protocols in network because

00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:50.360
network by its very nature they may connect various different people and as I

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:55.340
said various different people may have very different ideas about how things are

00:26:55.340 --> 00:27:01.040
to be done. Now, if people have very different ideas about how things are

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:07.840
to be done then, but they have to agree on some common basis otherwise they cannot

00:27:07.840 --> 00:27:14.660
communicate and this common basis is the protocol. So, there are actually there are

00:27:14.660 --> 00:27:19.900
number of protocols which are used in any network and we will be getting into that,

00:27:21.200 --> 00:27:25.580
but so you and I may have very different ideas about how some particular

00:27:25.580 --> 00:27:29.480
thing may be done. So, I may be doing this in a certain way you may be doing

00:27:29.480 --> 00:27:33.340
the same thing may be in a completely different way, but if you want to

00:27:33.340 --> 00:27:40.840
communicate we have to agree on some minimum protocol that is the protocol. So,

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:46.100
these protocols are the building blocks of this network architecture. So, each

00:27:46.100 --> 00:27:50.500
protocol object has two different interfaces. One is the service interface

00:27:51.160 --> 00:27:56.080
that defines operations on this protocol and peer to peer interface which

00:27:56.080 --> 00:28:01.060
defines messages exchanged with peer which means that for example, any

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:09.460
protocol so a protocol is let us say between peer. So, if in this diagram so these

00:28:09.460 --> 00:28:16.180
allies so there are so these two boxes allies they are the these two allies

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:23.300
they are the different I mean peers and they communicate with each other through

00:28:23.300 --> 00:28:28.180
this peer interface that is the protocol. Now, this protocol is supposed to

00:28:28.180 --> 00:28:31.640
achieve something is supposed to do something for this it requires something

00:28:31.640 --> 00:28:38.000
may be from some other so called layer. So, actually we have a layered

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:43.080
architecture and I am coming to that in the next slide. So, how this protocol is

00:28:43.080 --> 00:28:47.720
to be invoked etcetera for this we have a service interface and between the

00:28:47.720 --> 00:28:57.480
peers we have a peer interface. So, this is a so we have all these different

00:28:57.480 --> 00:29:02.000
layers. So, we have this n plus 1th layer and nth layer and n minus 1th layer in

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:07.660
general. So, actually these how they are to be layered about that also there are

00:29:07.660 --> 00:29:12.720
some standards and we will just look at two of them at this. So, let us say for

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:18.200
example, this networking business is broken up into I mean whatever the

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:23.000
jobs you have to do for achieving for smooth functioning of computer

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:29.000
network we break them up into different functionalities and these

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:34.520
functionalities are in different layers. Now, this is all in one place that

00:29:34.520 --> 00:29:39.180
means in one node similarly another node which is connected to the network

00:29:39.180 --> 00:29:46.040
will have its own layers and this at the same level let us say n and n

00:29:46.040 --> 00:29:52.940
here their peers. So, there will be a protocol between this n and n whereas

00:29:52.940 --> 00:29:56.460
there will be a service interface between the n plus 1th layer and nth layer

00:29:56.460 --> 00:30:00.140
similarly there may be a service interface between the nth layer and n minus

00:30:00.140 --> 00:30:06.460
1th layer and so on. So, let us see what they are like. So, as I said most

00:30:06.460 --> 00:30:12.500
networks are organized as series of layer the task of each layer is to

00:30:12.500 --> 00:30:16.500
give some service to the upper layer. So, that is the task of each

00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:20.600
layer and any layer maintains a virtual connection with the

00:30:20.600 --> 00:30:25.180
corresponding layer in a peer. So, this virtual connection any layer which

00:30:25.180 --> 00:30:29.740
maintains a virtual connection this virtual connection is used for

00:30:29.740 --> 00:30:35.360
running the peer to peer protocol whereas this is the service

00:30:35.360 --> 00:30:39.340
interface the task of each layer. So, each layer performs certain tasks

00:30:39.940 --> 00:30:44.220
of this whole networking business all right. So, each layer performs some task.

00:30:44.840 --> 00:30:51.620
So, this task is a service to the upper layer similarly for performing this

00:30:51.620 --> 00:30:55.860
task it may break it up into some sub task and some sub task may be

00:30:55.860 --> 00:30:59.680
delegated to a lower layer. So, this layer would have its own

00:30:59.680 --> 00:31:03.900
service interface to the lower layer whereas the its own task which

00:31:03.900 --> 00:31:06.640
are whatever I mean assuming that the sub task is done by the

00:31:06.640 --> 00:31:10.260
lower layer its own task is performed at the peer to peer level

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:19.060
using the corresponding protocol. So, there is peer to peer protocol

00:31:19.060 --> 00:31:22.060
running between any two corresponding and communicating layer the

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:25.840
interface between the layers in the same node is well defined.

00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:29.120
Well, another point is that the for this peer to peer protocol

00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:34.320
mostly they go through what is what I have just now called a

00:31:34.320 --> 00:31:38.660
virtual connection that means. So, what is a virtual connection

00:31:38.660 --> 00:31:45.020
virtual connection means that well physically physical connection we

00:31:45.020 --> 00:31:47.840
understand let us say two computers are connected by a wire.

00:31:48.420 --> 00:31:55.240
Now, as I mentioned earlier two computers may not I mean it is not

00:31:55.240 --> 00:31:59.260
necessary that the two computers need to be directly connected in

00:31:59.260 --> 00:32:02.800
order to communicate it may be an indirect connection also that

00:32:02.800 --> 00:32:06.100
means it is going through from one computer to another to

00:32:06.100 --> 00:32:09.260
another then it is hopping from node to node and then it reaches

00:32:09.260 --> 00:32:14.600
finally its destination. So, there is no direct physical

00:32:14.600 --> 00:32:18.920
communication although let us say when you are searching the

00:32:18.920 --> 00:32:21.760
internet let us say which is a very common experience these days.

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:26.080
So, you have connected to some computer which may be in the

00:32:26.080 --> 00:32:30.280
opposite part of the globe. So, to you it appears that you

00:32:30.280 --> 00:32:33.460
are sort of directly connected to that computer. So, whenever

00:32:33.460 --> 00:32:36.320
you are clicking something over here something happens which

00:32:36.320 --> 00:32:39.620
something happens means of course something happens. So, that

00:32:39.620 --> 00:32:44.600
you are clicking of the mouse somehow that gets communicated to that

00:32:44.600 --> 00:32:48.540
remote server remote web server and it may be the page

00:32:48.540 --> 00:32:52.620
changes or something. So, there is there seem to be a direct

00:32:52.620 --> 00:32:55.780
communication, but this communication is only virtual the

00:32:55.780 --> 00:32:58.700
physical communication is only at the lowest layer where

00:32:58.700 --> 00:33:02.540
physically something may be communicated by connected by

00:33:03.780 --> 00:33:06.760
piece of wire or it may be connected by fiber optic

00:33:06.760 --> 00:33:09.760
links or it may be connected by may be satellite links

00:33:09.760 --> 00:33:12.440
whatever it is. So, that is a that is the physical

00:33:12.440 --> 00:33:16.800
connection. So, that is why this for running all these

00:33:16.800 --> 00:33:20.120
protocols these peers need to communicate and this

00:33:20.120 --> 00:33:28.050
communicate is through a virtual connection. Now, the

00:33:28.050 --> 00:33:32.450
implementation of each layer in each node is transparent to

00:33:32.450 --> 00:33:39.350
other nodes. Now, actually this is the main point of having

00:33:39.350 --> 00:33:42.150
this layered architecture that the implementation of each

00:33:42.150 --> 00:33:49.090
layer in each node is transparent to the to the

00:33:49.090 --> 00:33:55.310
other to other nodes. As I said that I may want to do this

00:33:55.310 --> 00:33:59.510
one particular sub task in some some particular way I

00:33:59.510 --> 00:34:02.650
means let us say some company X and then some company Y

00:34:02.650 --> 00:34:05.930
wants to do the same thing may be in a different way.

00:34:06.430 --> 00:34:11.330
Now, you need not stop any of them. So, long as they agree

00:34:11.330 --> 00:34:16.450
on the protocol so long as they agree on the service

00:34:17.130 --> 00:34:20.390
service layers as what is the service interface between

00:34:20.390 --> 00:34:23.970
the different layers because upper layer service may be

00:34:23.970 --> 00:34:28.130
given by some product of some company whereas the

00:34:28.130 --> 00:34:33.370
lower layer of the lower layer is may be developed by

00:34:33.370 --> 00:34:36.490
some other company. Now, once again if they can

00:34:36.490 --> 00:34:39.710
decide on the interface between these two layers it

00:34:39.710 --> 00:34:42.170
is all right. Now, then they are very free to do

00:34:42.170 --> 00:34:46.210
it in their own way the same thing applies to the

00:34:46.210 --> 00:34:50.350
peers. For example, say two nodes say two

00:34:50.350 --> 00:34:52.650
particular layers in two particular nodes may be

00:34:52.650 --> 00:34:54.970
peer and let us say these nodes are computer themselves.

00:34:56.030 --> 00:34:59.170
Now, one may be a may be an IBM machine another may be

00:34:59.170 --> 00:35:02.530
a Sun machine or something and they may have they

00:35:02.530 --> 00:35:06.470
will have different operating system they will

00:35:06.470 --> 00:35:09.790
have different processors etcetera. But so far as

00:35:09.790 --> 00:35:13.190
the peer to peer protocol is concerned they agree.

00:35:13.710 --> 00:35:15.970
So, these are the messages I will send and then

00:35:15.970 --> 00:35:18.610
if I am expecting this kind of answer and he

00:35:18.610 --> 00:35:20.490
said these are the kinds of messages that I

00:35:20.490 --> 00:35:22.290
will accept and this kind of answer will be

00:35:22.290 --> 00:35:24.230
given etcetera etcetera. So, that is how a

00:35:24.230 --> 00:35:27.070
protocol goes. So, so long as they agree on this

00:35:27.070 --> 00:35:29.990
protocol these two companies are free to develop

00:35:29.990 --> 00:35:32.550
their product in their own way and when you

00:35:32.550 --> 00:35:35.310
allow that naturally people can innovate

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:37.270
people can put in different things. So, long

00:35:37.270 --> 00:35:41.270
as you are conforming to the standard of the

00:35:41.270 --> 00:35:44.110
protocol and the interfaces etcetera you

00:35:44.110 --> 00:35:46.550
can develop your own thing in a in your

00:35:46.550 --> 00:35:49.010
own way and that is very good for the

00:35:49.010 --> 00:35:51.710
development of the program of the of this

00:35:51.710 --> 00:35:54.070
entire network and the other point is that

00:35:54.070 --> 00:35:59.230
this is one way of abstracting out all

00:35:59.230 --> 00:36:03.110
these unnecessary details from the for

00:36:03.110 --> 00:36:06.350
example, any of these networking I mean

00:36:06.350 --> 00:36:08.030
say network operating system in a

00:36:08.030 --> 00:36:11.010
computer or a networking device they may

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:14.230
be very very complex indeed and some of

00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:16.630
the complexity is special to the

00:36:16.630 --> 00:36:20.710
implementer one implementer has decided to

00:36:20.710 --> 00:36:22.410
implement something in a some particular

00:36:22.410 --> 00:36:24.290
way and that will have a whole lot of details

00:36:24.290 --> 00:36:26.590
now we are not really interested in that

00:36:26.590 --> 00:36:28.410
one we want to abstract out of that.

00:36:29.050 --> 00:36:31.730
So, we concentrate on these protocols and

00:36:32.150 --> 00:36:34.250
the interfaces and the functionalities.

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:36.390
So, that gives us a fairly general

00:36:36.390 --> 00:36:40.630
picture about the entire networking I

00:36:41.390 --> 00:36:43.550
mean how networking is done and then

00:36:43.550 --> 00:36:46.950
if you are really if you go into business

00:36:46.950 --> 00:36:48.850
of developing some of these modules

00:36:48.850 --> 00:36:51.550
yourself then you have to go into some

00:36:52.290 --> 00:36:54.550
more nuts and bolts about how this service

00:36:55.250 --> 00:37:03.080
can be given. Now, the protocols between

00:37:03.080 --> 00:37:05.140
peer layers can be changed if the peers

00:37:05.140 --> 00:37:06.860
all agree naturally if you are changing

00:37:06.860 --> 00:37:08.380
the protocol all the peers have to

00:37:08.380 --> 00:37:09.960
agree otherwise the communication will

00:37:09.960 --> 00:37:12.620
break down. However, it need not be

00:37:12.620 --> 00:37:14.240
referred to other layer. So, that is

00:37:14.240 --> 00:37:15.640
another good thing about layering

00:37:15.640 --> 00:37:16.920
that means when I am changing

00:37:16.920 --> 00:37:20.000
something in this layer I may so long

00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:21.820
as I am not changing anything in the

00:37:21.820 --> 00:37:24.220
interface between the upper layer and

00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:26.240
this my layer or between myself and the

00:37:26.240 --> 00:37:27.900
lower layer so long as I am not changing

00:37:27.900 --> 00:37:29.900
anything in the interface internally

00:37:29.900 --> 00:37:31.900
whatever change I make will not matter.

00:37:32.120 --> 00:37:34.280
So, that is the good point of this

00:37:34.280 --> 00:37:36.740
layering and the service definition

00:37:36.740 --> 00:37:38.800
tells what the layer does and nothing

00:37:38.800 --> 00:37:40.920
else. That means nothing else means

00:37:40.920 --> 00:37:42.920
nothing about the specifics of

00:37:42.920 --> 00:37:44.980
implementation which may vary from

00:37:44.980 --> 00:37:49.040
one vendor to another vendor. The

00:37:49.040 --> 00:37:51.180
interface tells the process above it

00:37:51.180 --> 00:37:53.760
how to access it, it specifies what the

00:37:53.760 --> 00:37:56.240
parameters are and what results can be

00:37:56.240 --> 00:38:02.280
expected. So, we have two I mentioned

00:38:02.280 --> 00:38:05.860
here three models so three layered

00:38:05.860 --> 00:38:08.380
models the most famous is the OSI

00:38:08.380 --> 00:38:09.880
reference model which we will look

00:38:09.880 --> 00:38:12.120
into some of the details and then

00:38:12.120 --> 00:38:14.020
TCPIT reference model which is most

00:38:14.020 --> 00:38:16.900
widely used actually. So, OSI reference

00:38:16.900 --> 00:38:19.400
model I mean some part of it is

00:38:19.400 --> 00:38:21.540
somewhat theoretical because many

00:38:21.540 --> 00:38:24.820
people do not really do that and

00:38:24.820 --> 00:38:26.860
they do not consider that part to be

00:38:26.860 --> 00:38:29.300
very important. But TCPIT reference

00:38:29.300 --> 00:38:30.700
model is something which is almost

00:38:30.700 --> 00:38:34.660
ubiquitous and for ATM so the ATM is

00:38:34.660 --> 00:38:37.140
one networking technology which is

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:38.680
rather complete technology I mentioned

00:38:38.680 --> 00:38:41.160
here this here just as an example

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:42.860
so that is another kind of reference

00:38:42.860 --> 00:38:44.260
model. So, there are all kinds of

00:38:44.260 --> 00:38:48.080
different reference models sometimes

00:38:48.080 --> 00:38:50.780
these models so these models are

00:38:50.780 --> 00:38:52.500
basically a description of the

00:38:52.500 --> 00:38:53.820
different layers that are there

00:38:53.820 --> 00:38:56.380
and then naturally if you talk about

00:38:56.380 --> 00:38:57.800
the layers of what that layer is

00:38:57.800 --> 00:39:00.580
supposed to do that is what you say

00:39:00.580 --> 00:39:02.120
and then how it is to be

00:39:02.120 --> 00:39:03.700
interface with the layer above and

00:39:03.700 --> 00:39:06.560
the layer below. So, since this is

00:39:06.560 --> 00:39:08.280
just a number of layers one on top

00:39:08.280 --> 00:39:09.660
of the other they are also referred

00:39:09.660 --> 00:39:11.600
to as stacks sometimes. So, we call

00:39:11.600 --> 00:39:14.140
about the TCPIT stack and OSI stack

00:39:14.140 --> 00:39:16.540
and so on ATM stack. So, other

00:39:16.540 --> 00:39:18.460
protocol stacks exist and new ones

00:39:18.460 --> 00:39:21.240
are possible however to the extent

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:22.680
to which a particular model is

00:39:22.680 --> 00:39:24.760
universally accepted is the key

00:39:24.760 --> 00:39:26.820
to its success as I said that a

00:39:26.820 --> 00:39:28.300
lot of thought went into OSI

00:39:28.300 --> 00:39:30.260
reference model but in practice

00:39:30.260 --> 00:39:33.300
TCPIT became much more prevalent.

00:39:36.040 --> 00:39:38.780
So, let us see the OSI reference

00:39:38.780 --> 00:39:42.860
model this has seven layers and

00:39:42.860 --> 00:39:44.860
these layers are application layer

00:39:45.640 --> 00:39:47.880
the presentation layer the

00:39:47.880 --> 00:39:49.700
session layer the network layer

00:39:49.700 --> 00:39:51.440
the data link layer and the physical

00:39:51.440 --> 00:39:54.480
layer which means that in order

00:39:54.480 --> 00:39:58.600
that people can communicate

00:39:58.600 --> 00:40:00.620
over this network in a very

00:40:00.620 --> 00:40:03.040
seamless manner the all the

00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:05.880
jobs that are involved that

00:40:05.880 --> 00:40:08.180
have been broken down into seven

00:40:08.180 --> 00:40:09.460
parts and they are the different

00:40:09.460 --> 00:40:12.040
seven layers. So, application layer

00:40:12.040 --> 00:40:13.420
is something and then presentation

00:40:13.420 --> 00:40:15.460
session network data link and physical

00:40:15.460 --> 00:40:17.780
layer. So, each layer has some kind

00:40:17.780 --> 00:40:19.580
of functionality and all these

00:40:19.580 --> 00:40:21.760
functionalities together gives you

00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:24.240
the network overall functionality

00:40:24.240 --> 00:40:28.990
in the network. So, just a little

00:40:28.990 --> 00:40:30.970
bit more about peer level

00:40:30.970 --> 00:40:34.830
communication messages sent from

00:40:34.830 --> 00:40:36.130
one application to another

00:40:36.130 --> 00:40:38.070
application on different host. So, it

00:40:38.070 --> 00:40:39.650
travels down the layers on the

00:40:39.650 --> 00:40:42.230
sending machine and each layer adds

00:40:42.230 --> 00:40:43.890
a header to be used by its

00:40:43.890 --> 00:40:45.930
corresponding peer level and the

00:40:45.930 --> 00:40:47.290
bottom layer which is the physical

00:40:47.290 --> 00:40:48.890
layer sends the message to the

00:40:48.890 --> 00:40:51.170
TCP machine. So, this is the

00:40:51.990 --> 00:40:54.430
general scheme the point is

00:40:54.430 --> 00:40:56.990
that suppose we start something

00:40:56.990 --> 00:40:59.070
at the very top let us say in the

00:40:59.070 --> 00:41:00.990
case of OSI we call it the

00:41:00.990 --> 00:41:02.890
application layer. So, something

00:41:02.890 --> 00:41:04.930
has something some communication has

00:41:04.930 --> 00:41:08.530
been initiated at the application

00:41:08.530 --> 00:41:11.750
layer. Now, this application layer

00:41:11.750 --> 00:41:14.790
mind you is really it has its peer

00:41:14.790 --> 00:41:17.470
let us say in the destination machine.

00:41:18.190 --> 00:41:19.670
So, in the destination machine also

00:41:19.670 --> 00:41:21.690
some application layer program is

00:41:21.690 --> 00:41:24.110
running for example let us say you

00:41:24.110 --> 00:41:27.030
are doing a telnet kind of thing

00:41:27.030 --> 00:41:28.330
that means you are sort of logging

00:41:28.330 --> 00:41:29.530
on to another machine. So, you are

00:41:29.530 --> 00:41:31.950
doing a telnet to another machine. So,

00:41:32.210 --> 00:41:34.210
you will start your telnet program

00:41:34.210 --> 00:41:35.970
which will be a telnet client program

00:41:35.970 --> 00:41:38.170
and the destination machine

00:41:38.170 --> 00:41:40.990
will have will now the who will

00:41:41.970 --> 00:41:44.150
sort of respond to a telnet client

00:41:44.150 --> 00:41:45.990
only a telnet server we can respond

00:41:45.990 --> 00:41:48.090
to a telnet client this telnet

00:41:48.090 --> 00:41:49.970
server is in the remote machine.

00:41:50.530 --> 00:41:51.950
So, these are the two modules

00:41:51.950 --> 00:41:53.830
both of which are in the application

00:41:53.830 --> 00:41:56.210
layer. Now, how telnet is an example

00:41:56.210 --> 00:41:58.770
of a protocol this is an

00:41:58.770 --> 00:42:00.510
application layer protocol and

00:42:00.510 --> 00:42:02.050
what this protocol does how the

00:42:02.050 --> 00:42:04.870
telnet client will request how the

00:42:04.870 --> 00:42:07.030
telnet server will respond etcetera

00:42:07.030 --> 00:42:09.270
these are the internals of this

00:42:09.270 --> 00:42:10.790
particular protocol namely the

00:42:10.790 --> 00:42:14.310
telnet protocol. Now, so far as the

00:42:14.310 --> 00:42:15.790
telnet client or any of these

00:42:15.790 --> 00:42:17.210
application program is concerned

00:42:17.210 --> 00:42:19.130
when it tries to communicate to

00:42:19.130 --> 00:42:20.450
another application program in

00:42:20.450 --> 00:42:22.910
another machine it just knows

00:42:22.910 --> 00:42:24.770
about this protocol and how things

00:42:24.770 --> 00:42:26.490
are to be done at that layer. But

00:42:26.490 --> 00:42:28.490
how is it how is this communication

00:42:28.490 --> 00:42:30.310
to go from this machine to another

00:42:30.310 --> 00:42:32.470
machine for this if you put the

00:42:32.470 --> 00:42:34.090
whole thing in the same program then

00:42:34.090 --> 00:42:35.430
that becomes very complicated that

00:42:35.430 --> 00:42:36.950
was the idea of layering that means

00:42:36.950 --> 00:42:39.990
so given a sort of virtual connection.

00:42:40.630 --> 00:42:42.730
So, he will sort of for

00:42:42.730 --> 00:42:46.170
communicating to the target

00:42:46.170 --> 00:42:47.830
machine he will have some

00:42:47.830 --> 00:42:49.530
data to sort of same. So, that

00:42:49.530 --> 00:42:51.150
will add up to may be other

00:42:51.150 --> 00:42:52.790
kinds of data and then

00:42:52.790 --> 00:42:54.340
hand it over to the

00:42:54.990 --> 00:42:56.930
lower layer in the same node.

00:42:57.810 --> 00:42:59.390
Now, the lower layer similarly

00:42:59.390 --> 00:43:01.470
may be running a protocol with the

00:43:01.470 --> 00:43:03.090
corresponding layer in the

00:43:04.070 --> 00:43:06.370
remote machine. So, for that

00:43:06.370 --> 00:43:07.570
protocol it needs to do some

00:43:07.570 --> 00:43:09.270
communication. So, for that

00:43:09.270 --> 00:43:11.330
communication whatever message is

00:43:11.330 --> 00:43:12.990
to be sent here that get added

00:43:12.990 --> 00:43:15.330
to the original data which

00:43:15.330 --> 00:43:17.450
was sent by the application

00:43:17.450 --> 00:43:19.350
layer. So, this way as

00:43:19.350 --> 00:43:22.790
the message to be sent

00:43:23.390 --> 00:43:25.570
moves down the stack from

00:43:25.570 --> 00:43:27.510
the originating machine at

00:43:27.510 --> 00:43:29.390
each layer each layer is running

00:43:29.390 --> 00:43:31.270
its own protocol which is here. So, it

00:43:31.270 --> 00:43:33.110
has some message to the sort of

00:43:33.110 --> 00:43:35.610
add give to his pair. So, they

00:43:35.610 --> 00:43:37.430
will keep on adding it to this. So,

00:43:37.450 --> 00:43:39.230
this becomes fatter and fatter as it

00:43:39.230 --> 00:43:41.310
goes down. Now, as it goes down

00:43:41.310 --> 00:43:43.670
to the physical layer

00:43:43.670 --> 00:43:45.550
there it can now communicate

00:43:45.550 --> 00:43:47.470
to the remote machine and in

00:43:47.470 --> 00:43:49.070
the remote machine this will now

00:43:49.070 --> 00:43:50.950
again move up the stack and at

00:43:50.950 --> 00:43:52.830
each layer that particular

00:43:53.510 --> 00:43:55.450
the program or whatever that

00:43:55.450 --> 00:43:57.170
module in that layer he will

00:43:57.170 --> 00:43:58.990
take out the message which has been

00:43:58.990 --> 00:44:00.770
sent by its peer in the

00:44:01.950 --> 00:44:03.750
source. So, that is how

00:44:03.750 --> 00:44:05.430
communication will go on for

00:44:05.430 --> 00:44:11.910
all these protocols. So,

00:44:13.450 --> 00:44:13.970
sending

00:44:13.970 --> 00:44:16.690
a message. So,

00:44:16.710 --> 00:44:17.990
received on the receiving side

00:44:17.990 --> 00:44:19.610
and it is passed up through each

00:44:19.610 --> 00:44:21.430
layer and each layer reads the

00:44:21.430 --> 00:44:24.250
corresponding header. That

00:44:24.250 --> 00:44:25.570
means the corresponding header which

00:44:25.570 --> 00:44:27.130
has been sent by

00:44:27.130 --> 00:44:29.190
the by its own peer

00:44:29.190 --> 00:44:31.090
on the from the source

00:44:31.090 --> 00:44:35.410
machine. So, this is an

00:44:35.410 --> 00:44:37.030
example. Let us say

00:44:37.030 --> 00:44:38.670
we have two machines

00:44:38.670 --> 00:44:40.910
and one machine is a DOS

00:44:40.910 --> 00:44:42.810
machine. That means it is

00:44:42.810 --> 00:44:45.370
some DOS is of course

00:44:45.370 --> 00:44:46.830
an old operating system which

00:44:46.830 --> 00:44:48.410
used to run on PCs and

00:44:48.410 --> 00:44:49.950
which was there from the

00:44:49.950 --> 00:44:51.910
Microsoft DOS and the PC DOS

00:44:51.910 --> 00:44:53.990
etc. IBM DOS was there and

00:44:53.990 --> 00:44:55.550
Macintosh was a different company

00:44:55.550 --> 00:44:57.470
as I mentioned. So, these two

00:44:57.470 --> 00:44:59.270
can still communicate that means

00:44:59.270 --> 00:45:00.690
they are two different machines.

00:45:01.670 --> 00:45:03.650
But this original data

00:45:03.650 --> 00:45:05.090
which was there which was

00:45:05.090 --> 00:45:06.750
which sort of originated from the

00:45:06.750 --> 00:45:08.890
application layer. Now, there is a

00:45:08.890 --> 00:45:10.110
presentation layer. Now, this

00:45:10.110 --> 00:45:12.050
presentation layer will add its

00:45:12.050 --> 00:45:14.410
own presentation header to this

00:45:14.410 --> 00:45:16.530
original data. Now, this whole

00:45:16.530 --> 00:45:18.630
thing becomes sort of data

00:45:18.630 --> 00:45:20.310
for the next layer namely the

00:45:20.310 --> 00:45:21.510
session layer. So, session layer

00:45:21.510 --> 00:45:24.650
will add its own header. So, as

00:45:24.650 --> 00:45:26.070
I said that the original data

00:45:26.070 --> 00:45:28.030
becomes fatter and fatter

00:45:28.030 --> 00:45:29.850
and fatter till it comes to

00:45:29.850 --> 00:45:31.670
the physical layer. Now, at the

00:45:31.670 --> 00:45:34.490
physical layer data is

00:45:34.490 --> 00:45:36.090
actually sent let us say in

00:45:36.090 --> 00:45:37.790
one hop to the destination

00:45:38.570 --> 00:45:40.630
machine. Now, here as

00:45:40.630 --> 00:45:42.530
this whole fat packet

00:45:42.530 --> 00:45:44.850
travels up each

00:45:44.850 --> 00:45:46.110
layer will strip

00:45:46.110 --> 00:45:47.790
off its corresponding header

00:45:47.790 --> 00:45:49.110
which is coming from its peer

00:45:49.110 --> 00:45:50.970
layer in the source. So, that is

00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:53.230
how each of the peers

00:45:53.230 --> 00:45:54.990
can communicate and each of

00:45:54.990 --> 00:45:56.370
them has a virtual connection

00:45:56.370 --> 00:45:58.410
with the destination

00:45:58.410 --> 00:46:00.410
machine although the actual

00:46:00.410 --> 00:46:01.490
connection is the physical

00:46:01.490 --> 00:46:09.270
connection. So, these are

00:46:09.270 --> 00:46:11.010
the seven protocol

00:46:11.010 --> 00:46:12.130
layers. Now, we are going

00:46:12.130 --> 00:46:13.910
bottom up and I will just tell

00:46:13.910 --> 00:46:15.410
you in brief later on I will

00:46:15.410 --> 00:46:18.090
just go down to how they are

00:46:18.090 --> 00:46:19.690
sort of some more details

00:46:19.690 --> 00:46:21.550
about each of these layer.

00:46:22.310 --> 00:46:23.570
So, first is the physical link

00:46:23.570 --> 00:46:25.470
which is how to

00:46:27.290 --> 00:46:28.430
transmit the bits

00:46:31.270 --> 00:46:33.450
and then we have

00:46:33.450 --> 00:46:35.710
the data link layer that is

00:46:35.710 --> 00:46:37.550
how to transmit frames. Now, what is

00:46:37.550 --> 00:46:39.030
the difference between transmitting bits

00:46:39.030 --> 00:46:40.570
and transmitting frames we will see

00:46:40.570 --> 00:46:43.070
to that, but a data link

00:46:43.490 --> 00:46:45.710
basically is a direct connection

00:46:45.710 --> 00:46:47.590
that means how two computers

00:46:47.590 --> 00:46:49.670
which are directly or two nodes which

00:46:49.670 --> 00:46:51.130
are directly connected to each other

00:46:51.130 --> 00:46:52.850
how they will communicate that is a

00:46:52.850 --> 00:46:54.430
matter of data link layer.

00:46:55.570 --> 00:46:57.410
Now, as mentioned earlier

00:46:57.410 --> 00:46:59.310
computers need not be directly

00:46:59.310 --> 00:47:00.870
connected to each other in order

00:47:00.870 --> 00:47:02.250
to communicate they have to be

00:47:02.250 --> 00:47:03.850
connected to the network somehow

00:47:03.850 --> 00:47:06.450
or other. So, if there is

00:47:06.450 --> 00:47:08.110
there are say two machines A and

00:47:08.110 --> 00:47:09.670
B or two nodes A and B which

00:47:09.670 --> 00:47:11.510
want to communicate and there is

00:47:11.510 --> 00:47:13.130
no direct link between A and

00:47:13.130 --> 00:47:15.090
B. So, it may go via C, D,

00:47:15.290 --> 00:47:16.870
E and then B all right.

00:47:17.350 --> 00:47:18.770
Now, how do you know

00:47:18.770 --> 00:47:21.190
that you have to go through

00:47:21.190 --> 00:47:23.050
C, D, E and then you will

00:47:23.050 --> 00:47:25.110
actually reach B. So, somebody has

00:47:25.110 --> 00:47:27.170
to keep track of the route

00:47:27.170 --> 00:47:28.910
how to route the packets over the

00:47:28.910 --> 00:47:30.910
entire network. So, that is the job

00:47:30.910 --> 00:47:32.050
of the network layer.

00:47:33.010 --> 00:47:34.790
Then there is a transport layer that

00:47:34.790 --> 00:47:36.390
means how to send packets to the

00:47:36.390 --> 00:47:38.410
application. Now, we have been talking

00:47:38.410 --> 00:47:40.270
about packets a packet is a

00:47:40.270 --> 00:47:42.610
sort of some data which is sort of

00:47:42.610 --> 00:47:44.390
has been segregated and put into

00:47:44.390 --> 00:47:46.270
a sort of package together.

00:47:47.370 --> 00:47:48.010
So, that is a packet,

00:47:49.750 --> 00:47:50.770
but the original

00:47:50.770 --> 00:47:52.490
application layer need not

00:47:52.490 --> 00:47:54.170
have any concept of any packet.

00:47:54.330 --> 00:47:56.090
So, somebody has to packetize all this

00:47:56.090 --> 00:47:57.930
data and there are may be other

00:47:57.930 --> 00:47:59.370
functions in the transport layer.

00:47:59.490 --> 00:48:01.510
So, that is the they make packets

00:48:01.510 --> 00:48:03.210
out of the data given by the

00:48:03.210 --> 00:48:05.510
upper layer. So, that is the

00:48:05.510 --> 00:48:07.810
transport layer. Then the session

00:48:07.810 --> 00:48:09.910
layer which manages the virtual

00:48:09.910 --> 00:48:11.710
connection between in the of the

00:48:11.710 --> 00:48:13.370
application layer which manages the

00:48:13.370 --> 00:48:15.610
virtual connection and some

00:48:15.610 --> 00:48:17.230
properties of this connection we

00:48:17.230 --> 00:48:19.490
will talk about it. The presentation

00:48:19.490 --> 00:48:21.290
layer that means how do you encode

00:48:21.290 --> 00:48:23.150
and decode messages that means two

00:48:23.750 --> 00:48:25.670
different machines may have

00:48:25.670 --> 00:48:27.330
two different ways of encoding

00:48:27.330 --> 00:48:29.970
things. So, there is that is

00:48:29.970 --> 00:48:31.470
the job of the presentation layer

00:48:31.470 --> 00:48:33.250
as well as the security comes

00:48:33.250 --> 00:48:35.510
here and application. So,

00:48:35.510 --> 00:48:37.510
whatever the actual job the user is

00:48:37.510 --> 00:48:39.110
interested in the human user that

00:48:39.110 --> 00:48:41.110
is. So, that comes in the

00:48:41.110 --> 00:48:42.170
application layer.

00:48:43.970 --> 00:48:45.450
So, the application layer

00:48:45.450 --> 00:48:47.270
contains a variety of protocols.

00:48:47.610 --> 00:48:48.970
So, it depends on what you are

00:48:48.970 --> 00:48:51.110
trying to do. So, the various

00:48:51.110 --> 00:48:52.670
users may want to do various things

00:48:52.670 --> 00:48:55.210
examples are FTP, Telnet,

00:48:55.770 --> 00:48:57.510
SMTP, HTTP, etcetera.

00:48:57.730 --> 00:48:58.910
So, these are the names of some

00:48:58.910 --> 00:49:01.110
protocols. By the FTP protocol

00:49:01.110 --> 00:49:02.470
what you can do is that you

00:49:02.470 --> 00:49:05.190
can download files from another

00:49:05.190 --> 00:49:07.390
machine. By HTTP you can

00:49:07.390 --> 00:49:09.070
look at web pages,

00:49:09.510 --> 00:49:11.410
surf, etcetera. There is a

00:49:11.410 --> 00:49:13.010
protocol called SMTP

00:49:13.010 --> 00:49:14.750
which you use for sending

00:49:14.750 --> 00:49:16.610
mails. Now, you may not

00:49:16.610 --> 00:49:18.750
be you may not have come

00:49:18.750 --> 00:49:20.110
across this protocol directly,

00:49:20.110 --> 00:49:21.550
but the point is that whenever

00:49:21.550 --> 00:49:23.210
let us say you are sending a mail

00:49:24.430 --> 00:49:25.890
from let us say an

00:49:25.890 --> 00:49:27.850
unique machine or something you

00:49:27.850 --> 00:49:30.130
are using the SMTP protocol

00:49:30.130 --> 00:49:31.950
whether or not you know it. So, SMTP

00:49:31.950 --> 00:49:34.030
protocol has been built into that.

00:49:34.410 --> 00:49:36.170
Similarly, when you

00:49:36.170 --> 00:49:37.310
are Telneting that means the

00:49:37.310 --> 00:49:39.410
Telnet server and the Telnet client,

00:49:39.550 --> 00:49:40.950
etcetera. They will be there in your

00:49:40.950 --> 00:49:43.310
machine as a part of the OS maybe.

00:49:45.030 --> 00:49:46.090
So, the application there

00:49:46.090 --> 00:49:47.930
usually requires reliable and cheap

00:49:47.930 --> 00:49:49.330
connection to its pier. That is

00:49:49.330 --> 00:49:50.930
what the application layer what it

00:49:50.930 --> 00:49:52.370
is concerned about that it must be

00:49:52.370 --> 00:49:54.690
connected reliably and cheaply to

00:49:54.690 --> 00:49:56.990
its pier. So, examples of pier

00:49:56.990 --> 00:49:59.410
we have. So, given some

00:49:59.410 --> 00:50:00.870
nodes, given service, etcetera.

00:50:02.790 --> 00:50:03.790
Now, application layer

00:50:03.790 --> 00:50:06.630
hence this whatever it

00:50:06.630 --> 00:50:07.630
has to send to the

00:50:07.630 --> 00:50:09.370
presentation layer which handles

00:50:09.370 --> 00:50:10.930
as I said the format of the

00:50:10.930 --> 00:50:12.730
data. Protocol conversion,

00:50:13.330 --> 00:50:14.550
data translation whether it is

00:50:14.550 --> 00:50:16.210
ASCII or maybe some other

00:50:16.210 --> 00:50:18.250
eBigBig or something to data

00:50:18.250 --> 00:50:19.970
compression, data encryption. These

00:50:19.970 --> 00:50:21.550
are handled in the presentation

00:50:21.550 --> 00:50:24.610
layer. In the session

00:50:24.610 --> 00:50:26.450
layer it allows applications and

00:50:26.450 --> 00:50:28.290
different computers to share a

00:50:28.290 --> 00:50:29.550
connection. Now, what do I mean by

00:50:29.550 --> 00:50:31.250
connection? The point is that later

00:50:31.250 --> 00:50:33.350
on just as I go to the next layer

00:50:33.350 --> 00:50:35.090
you will see that I will

00:50:35.090 --> 00:50:38.550
packetize this data. But then

00:50:38.550 --> 00:50:40.390
so far as the user is concerned

00:50:40.390 --> 00:50:42.470
he wants a continuous connection.

00:50:42.950 --> 00:50:44.710
So, this connection is handled

00:50:44.710 --> 00:50:46.070
in the session layer. So, it

00:50:46.070 --> 00:50:47.370
can provide checkpoints. That

00:50:47.370 --> 00:50:48.710
means if there is some disruption

00:50:48.710 --> 00:50:50.590
you may come back to it and

00:50:50.590 --> 00:50:52.930
sort of get the original

00:50:52.930 --> 00:50:54.930
something original state

00:50:54.930 --> 00:50:56.270
back and then you can

00:50:56.270 --> 00:50:57.650
retransmit if there is some

00:50:57.650 --> 00:50:59.510
dialogue control of who can

00:50:59.510 --> 00:51:02.210
transmit etcetera. The

00:51:02.210 --> 00:51:03.650
transport layer the basic function

00:51:03.650 --> 00:51:05.270
of the transport layer is to accept

00:51:05.270 --> 00:51:07.370
data from the layer above, split

00:51:07.370 --> 00:51:09.250
it into smaller units if necessary.

00:51:09.450 --> 00:51:11.010
Now, why we need to split that we

00:51:11.010 --> 00:51:12.130
will come to that later on.

00:51:12.390 --> 00:51:14.390
So, these are the packets so to

00:51:14.390 --> 00:51:16.310
say pass this to the network layer

00:51:16.310 --> 00:51:18.770
and ensure that the pieces

00:51:18.770 --> 00:51:20.450
all arrive correctly and in the

00:51:20.450 --> 00:51:22.290
right order at the other end. For

00:51:22.290 --> 00:51:23.770
example, you have chopped them into pieces

00:51:23.770 --> 00:51:25.930
now they may become out of order.

00:51:26.130 --> 00:51:28.050
So, that is the business of the transport layer.

00:51:28.650 --> 00:51:29.890
This should also be done in a

00:51:29.890 --> 00:51:31.390
cheap and efficient manner etcetera

00:51:31.390 --> 00:51:33.150
the same thing applies.

00:51:34.390 --> 00:51:35.970
So, the type or there may be

00:51:35.970 --> 00:51:37.590
different types of transport services.

00:51:37.790 --> 00:51:39.570
So, there may be multiple protocols

00:51:39.570 --> 00:51:41.050
here. One could be error free

00:51:41.050 --> 00:51:43.410
point to point channels or it

00:51:43.410 --> 00:51:46.030
could be a very cheap kind of channel

00:51:46.030 --> 00:51:48.350
or it may broadcasting of messages

00:51:48.350 --> 00:51:49.670
to multiple destinations.

00:51:49.970 --> 00:51:50.990
So, these are the different types

00:51:50.990 --> 00:51:53.090
of transport services which are

00:51:53.090 --> 00:51:56.900
possible. The network layer as I

00:51:56.900 --> 00:51:58.660
said it decides on what route

00:51:58.660 --> 00:52:00.500
to take locally. So, that the

00:52:00.500 --> 00:52:02.400
intended message ultimately reaches

00:52:02.400 --> 00:52:04.800
the destination. It controls

00:52:04.800 --> 00:52:06.420
broadcasting by essentially

00:52:06.420 --> 00:52:08.740
segregating the different networks etcetera

00:52:08.740 --> 00:52:10.980
it handles technological mismatches.

00:52:11.540 --> 00:52:12.740
So, we will get into the details

00:52:12.740 --> 00:52:14.900
of this later on. It does congestion

00:52:14.900 --> 00:52:17.180
control. It had the handles

00:52:17.180 --> 00:52:19.060
billing information etcetera.

00:52:20.940 --> 00:52:21.920
And the data link layer

00:52:21.920 --> 00:52:23.360
make the physical layer appear

00:52:23.360 --> 00:52:25.060
like a channel that is pure transmission

00:52:25.060 --> 00:52:26.780
errors. Actually in the physical

00:52:26.780 --> 00:52:28.680
layer there may be errors, but the

00:52:28.680 --> 00:52:30.420
data link layer handles this error

00:52:30.420 --> 00:52:32.360
correction etcetera at the very

00:52:32.360 --> 00:52:34.580
lowest level. And finally, at

00:52:34.580 --> 00:52:36.120
the lowest level we have the

00:52:36.120 --> 00:52:37.560
physical layer where data is

00:52:37.560 --> 00:52:39.680
actually transmitted as raw bits

00:52:39.680 --> 00:52:40.120
etcetera.

00:52:41.980 --> 00:52:43.820
The other layer is TCPIP reference

00:52:43.820 --> 00:52:45.320
model where the session and the

00:52:45.320 --> 00:52:47.220
presentation layer are not there. So,

00:52:47.260 --> 00:52:48.400
we have the application layer,

00:52:48.560 --> 00:52:50.480
transport layer, network layer and

00:52:50.480 --> 00:52:52.000
maybe something like the data link

00:52:52.000 --> 00:52:53.720
or the physical layer. So, the raw

00:52:53.720 --> 00:52:55.360
correspondence is something like

00:52:55.360 --> 00:52:56.960
this. We have the TCPIP model

00:52:56.960 --> 00:52:59.360
where we have just these two and

00:52:59.360 --> 00:53:01.480
you have the OSI reference model

00:53:01.480 --> 00:53:03.500
with so many. So, more or less

00:53:03.500 --> 00:53:05.200
they match the essential functions

00:53:05.200 --> 00:53:08.200
match. And there are other kinds

00:53:08.200 --> 00:53:10.740
of protocols which are also

00:53:10.740 --> 00:53:11.880
used, but the

00:53:11.880 --> 00:53:13.780
TCPIP and the OSI these are

00:53:13.780 --> 00:53:14.680
the most common.

00:53:15.140 --> 00:53:17.160
In the next class we will be

00:53:17.160 --> 00:53:19.060
discussing the different

00:53:19.060 --> 00:53:21.280
structures of network. So, today

00:53:21.280 --> 00:53:22.840
we just had an abstract view of

00:53:22.840 --> 00:53:24.520
network. In the next class we

00:53:24.520 --> 00:53:26.380
will handle the different structures.

00:53:42.530 --> 00:53:44.430
Good day. So,

00:53:45.130 --> 00:53:46.350
in this lecture

00:53:46.350 --> 00:53:47.910
we will discuss

00:53:47.910 --> 00:53:49.690
the network

00:53:49.690 --> 00:53:50.710
topology.

00:53:52.530 --> 00:53:54.250
Now, what do we

00:53:54.250 --> 00:53:55.550
mean by a network

00:53:55.550 --> 00:54:07.790
topology? So, let us

00:54:08.160 --> 00:54:09.660
just have a quick

00:54:09.660 --> 00:54:11.280
recap of

00:54:11.280 --> 00:54:13.580
what we had learned last

00:54:13.580 --> 00:54:15.360
time. Basically a

00:54:15.360 --> 00:54:16.880
computer network

00:54:17.410 --> 00:54:19.360
would be a number of nodes

00:54:19.360 --> 00:54:21.260
which are connected by some

00:54:21.260 --> 00:54:22.320
communication links.

00:54:23.460 --> 00:54:25.560
So, there is some kind

00:54:25.560 --> 00:54:27.440
of what we usually

00:54:27.440 --> 00:54:28.260
call a graph.

00:54:29.260 --> 00:54:31.520
So, this graph has certain structure.

00:54:32.280 --> 00:54:33.480
So, we talk about the structure.

00:54:33.640 --> 00:54:35.640
Now, this structure has an implication

00:54:35.640 --> 00:54:37.560
about how we will go

00:54:37.560 --> 00:54:38.520
about communicating.

00:54:39.440 --> 00:54:41.320
As I said that it is in general

00:54:41.320 --> 00:54:43.560
not feasible to have one to

00:54:43.560 --> 00:54:45.280
one communication between

00:54:45.280 --> 00:54:47.520
each pair of nodes. That is not

00:54:47.520 --> 00:54:49.380
possible at all. So, what kind

00:54:49.380 --> 00:54:51.460
of structure that means there

00:54:51.460 --> 00:54:53.480
is nodes connected in which manner

00:54:53.480 --> 00:54:54.920
that is the

00:54:55.480 --> 00:54:57.260
subject matter of discussion today.

00:54:59.640 --> 00:55:00.440
So,

00:55:01.120 --> 00:55:01.960
a network may be

00:55:01.960 --> 00:55:03.660
represented as a graph as I said

00:55:03.660 --> 00:55:05.320
nodes representing computers

00:55:05.320 --> 00:55:07.100
or network devices like switches

00:55:07.100 --> 00:55:08.900
routers etcetera

00:55:09.480 --> 00:55:11.380
and the links represent communication

00:55:11.380 --> 00:55:12.000
links.

00:55:12.860 --> 00:55:15.120
Modes of communication may be broadcast

00:55:15.120 --> 00:55:17.140
or point to point.

00:55:17.360 --> 00:55:19.180
We have discussed this.

00:55:22.640 --> 00:55:23.100
Now,

00:55:25.460 --> 00:55:27.000
there are various

00:55:27.000 --> 00:55:28.400
first let us talk

00:55:28.400 --> 00:55:30.780
about the so called LAN topologies.

00:55:31.700 --> 00:55:32.300
By

00:55:32.300 --> 00:55:34.440
LAN you remember is the

00:55:34.440 --> 00:55:35.800
local area network

00:55:36.360 --> 00:55:38.700
and local area network topologies

00:55:38.700 --> 00:55:39.600
are

00:55:40.300 --> 00:55:41.980
three are very common star,

00:55:42.400 --> 00:55:43.520
ring and a bus.

00:55:44.920 --> 00:55:46.280
So, we will just

00:55:46.280 --> 00:55:47.640
look at all of them.

00:55:51.090 --> 00:55:51.670
So,

00:55:51.750 --> 00:55:54.030
first of all we first

00:55:54.030 --> 00:55:56.350
take up this bus topology

00:55:56.350 --> 00:55:58.470
and why do we take up a bus topology.

00:55:59.030 --> 00:56:00.110
So, this is a very simple

00:56:00.110 --> 00:56:01.010
kind of network.

00:56:01.850 --> 00:56:04.090
So, this is based on

00:56:04.090 --> 00:56:05.550
a shared broadcast

00:56:05.550 --> 00:56:06.270
link.

00:56:07.490 --> 00:56:07.810
So, this

00:56:09.090 --> 00:56:10.750
we want to still want to do

00:56:10.750 --> 00:56:13.230
point to point communication. That means

00:56:13.230 --> 00:56:15.770
there may be so many nodes a,

00:56:15.990 --> 00:56:17.030
b, c, d etcetera

00:56:17.870 --> 00:56:18.870
which are

00:56:20.510 --> 00:56:21.930
which are connected to the

00:56:21.930 --> 00:56:23.910
network and a wants

00:56:23.910 --> 00:56:25.010
to communicate to b.

00:56:25.610 --> 00:56:27.670
So, c, d are connected

00:56:27.670 --> 00:56:29.710
that is all right, but a specifically wants

00:56:29.710 --> 00:56:31.070
to communicate to b.

00:56:32.150 --> 00:56:33.870
So, each pair of communicating nodes

00:56:33.870 --> 00:56:35.830
use the link for a short

00:56:35.830 --> 00:56:37.770
time. So, a uses the link

00:56:37.770 --> 00:56:39.770
for a short time to send this message to b.

00:56:40.230 --> 00:56:41.450
Then after that is over

00:56:41.450 --> 00:56:43.730
then maybe c might send

00:56:43.730 --> 00:56:45.430
something to d or something like that.

00:56:46.570 --> 00:56:47.910
So, other nodes ignore

00:56:49.010 --> 00:56:50.050
the communication.

00:56:50.050 --> 00:56:52.230
So, since this is shared broadcast

00:56:52.230 --> 00:56:53.590
link all the links

00:56:53.590 --> 00:56:55.510
all the nodes

00:56:56.050 --> 00:56:58.250
get the communication.

00:56:58.570 --> 00:56:59.910
So, that is not private in that

00:56:59.910 --> 00:57:01.570
sense. So, all the nodes get

00:57:01.570 --> 00:57:04.070
that communication, but they usually

00:57:04.070 --> 00:57:05.870
will ignore this communication.

00:57:06.290 --> 00:57:08.670
Whereas, a b will

00:57:08.670 --> 00:57:10.310
sort of copy this for

00:57:10.310 --> 00:57:11.470
its own purpose.

00:57:12.850 --> 00:57:14.310
Now, there has to be

00:57:14.310 --> 00:57:16.230
a distributed protocol to decide

00:57:16.230 --> 00:57:18.210
who gets to use the

00:57:18.210 --> 00:57:20.550
link. There has to be some protocol.

00:57:20.770 --> 00:57:22.150
Otherwise, if a

00:57:22.150 --> 00:57:23.990
wants to communicate with b

00:57:23.990 --> 00:57:25.890
and b wants to say c wants

00:57:25.890 --> 00:57:27.010
to communicate with d,

00:57:27.870 --> 00:57:29.930
their communications will go and collide

00:57:29.930 --> 00:57:31.950
in the shared broadcast

00:57:31.950 --> 00:57:34.010
medium. So, the communication of

00:57:34.010 --> 00:57:36.110
both the parties of that

00:57:36.110 --> 00:57:37.570
means both the pairs of nodes

00:57:38.150 --> 00:57:39.170
they will get garbled.

00:57:40.310 --> 00:57:42.150
So, that I mean and sometimes

00:57:42.150 --> 00:57:44.350
actually that will happen

00:57:44.350 --> 00:57:45.970
that the things will get garbled,

00:57:45.970 --> 00:57:47.490
but that is not what we

00:57:48.070 --> 00:57:48.750
want to.

